[Music]  isabella monroe just started a new job  working as a development coordinator at  west group properties in vancouver  and she loves it i just honestly  couldn't be happier where i am she's  busy and not looking for a new job but  she keeps getting contacted with other  offers everywhere is hiring right now  everybody is very desperate for people  and i often get linkedin messages which  is a big change for her from just a  couple of years ago to be honest before  the pandemic i was just feeling lucky to  have a job a labor shortage due to  declining birth rates and an aging  population is creating a major shift in  the canadian job market there's now a  war for talent with companies battling  to attract workers in all industries  from hospitality to construction to  banking i think we're at an  unprecedented time where the power  balance between employers and employees  has shifted job postings in canada  overall are up 60 from pre-pandemic so  if you look at data from canada there's  about a million job openings as of right  now there's in fact just about as many  job openings as there are people looking  for jobs  in order to attract candidates companies  need to know what matters to people like  isabella  is that good to john hi come on in and  what it's going to take to win them over  i think right now options are everything  and especially with everybody hiring  right now it's it's really important to  me  but what exactly are the right options  to offer is it higher pay we're happy  better benefits or something else  entirely for isabella it's culture  personally for work it's never been a  clocking clock out kind of job but even  more so now i'm i want a community and i  want a good culture in the company that  i work for that it's not just work it's  outside of work as well  the exposure will be in all units  stephen dollywall works in sales they've  been in business for about 70 years a  year ago when he was looking for a job  he applied for positions and got no  response the market was so competitive  at the time with many people looking for  a job but not that many openings i was  really tough to to find the right fit he  ended up landing a job as a sales  associate seven months ago at the same  company as isabella two beds one basket  started  eight hundred thousand he was looking  for a good work life balance and found  it here we have set hours and we aren't  expected to be available during outside  of our work hours which normally in the  real estate industry are kind of  available all the time so in that sense  you know you really get a chance to step  back from work enjoy the rest of your  life now with the demand for workers  he's being contacted by those employers  who didn't respond to his job  applications a year ago recently i've  had some of those same positions i  applied for now reach out to me  to see if i was still interested in  pursuing that opportunity  alicia shikoloni is the vice president  of people and culture for west group i  think  good talent has their pick in terms of  their employer and we are well aware of  that  to be competitive the company offers  perks like catered lunches gym  memberships and paid volunteer days  employees can leave together and go work  out at a local gym and that's paid for  by west group and we also offer  discounts at local gyms and personal  training and if employees complete 20  workouts they get a free day off  wash dryers here full size  and they have an open door policy for  employees to engage directly with upper  management which means a lot to steven i  feel just as valued as some of our  executives it feels like we're all  pulling in the right direction at the  same time and each of us have our own  part to do  to accomplish our greater goal brian  cropp is chief of research at gartner's  hr practice based out of arlington  virginia he's been researching the shift  in hiring and employment we survey about  a million people or so per year  we download every job description that's  posted online and then analyze that to  understand what employers are looking  for within the labor market what can you  tell me about what people are looking  for when it comes to their work  now versus pre-pandemic first kind of  the most obvious one is that employees  now  expect flexibility that by far and away  is the biggest change now what a lot of  employers think it means is that  flexibility is really just about place  do i work from an office or do i work  from home but from an employee  perspective flexibility is not just  about place but it's also about when i  work can i start working earlier in the  day take a two or three hour break  during the middle of the day because  that meets my needs working from home or  hybrid work was barely on the radar for  many companies pre-pandemic now it's  something the majority of people want  and for some flexibility is more  important than pay  james spearing is the director of talent  acquisition for scotiabank in toronto  and he's currently looking to fill  hundreds of positions  in terms of the things that employees  are looking for does that jive with your  experience we found that  flexibility is super important  very critical and also compensation  depending on the area of course that's  always critical um because that's sort  of you know our financial value but the  concepts around flexibility of work and  culture are two of the most important  employee value propositions for any  talent segment and that's globally it  will take us years to really figure out  how to run a workforce with remote  employees and hybrid employees brian's  research shows the flexibility to work  from home also means employers have to  rethink their perceptions of their  workforce even though all the  performance and productivity data  indicate that an employee is working  remote is just as productive if not more  so compared to employee comes into the  office but managers disagree with that  fact about two-thirds of managers say  they're more likely to promote an  employee comes into the office and more  likely to give an employee who comes  into the office a higher rate  and that would impact specific groups  employers are trying to attract women  are more likely to work remote or hyper  than men  if we do nothing we will have  discrimination against women in a remote  and hybrid world we see a similar trend  with people of color that they're more  likely to want to work remote or hybrid  than other employees and if we do  nothing there we'll have less diverse  leadership pipelines we'll have a less  diverse organization and that will  directly translate to lower performing  organizations that are there in order to  attract a diverse group of candidates  companies are modernizing where and how  they find new people scotiabank has  opened satellite offices in vancouver  and ottawa and it's removed resumes as a  requirement  starting in the fall of 2020 actually we  went resume less for our campus and  early talent entry points across the  bank in canada we realized that what  we're looking for actually at the campus  or early talent level is skills and  traits that will set people up for the  future and we wanted to make sure that  we could look at talent in a different  way they're using a new approach with  the help of recruitment company plum it  offers a 25-minute survey for potential  candidates caitlin mcgregor is the ceo  of the waterloo-based company if you  look at the data that's in resumes  it's full of systemic barriers and  biases that dictate access to education  access to even internships or how fast  somebody's progressed in their career  you can line up 100 people and say okay  these people tomorrow would be eligible  they could do the job but it won't tell  you who out of those hundred people will  actually perform well be a top performer  enjoy the work  however a job that's a good fit is only  part of the solution employees also need  to feel appreciated respected and  understood i think that was actually one  of the most important things for me when  i was looking for a new job is that  having an employer who actually  recognizes my hard work i'm really happy  to put in the hours but i would like to  be recognized one of the biggest things  that we've learned throughout the  pandemic is that we don't have workers  working for us we have human beings that  have real lives real concerns real  families real issues that are impacting  them and the best organizations realize  that if you treat your employees like  people not like workers not only do you  get higher performing employees who are  more likely to stay it's just also the  right thing to do because it creates  better lives for those employees it was  very one way the question that remains  is the shift to accommodate workers in  ways like hybrid work something that  will stick around  i think this is a shift that's permanent  not temporary and the world that we're  going into will be hybrid and remote for  the as long as the eye can see at this  point based on every expectation every  productivity measure every finance  measure every technology measure  this appears to be the world that we're  going to be in for the foreseeable  future brian believes the support  workers are getting now will directly  translate into more successful  businesses a win for the employers and a  win for employees it's great to feel in  demand and have a lot of control over  where i can go with my career and to be  very selective with what i'm looking for  and to find the best opportunity for  myself i want to be doing something that  i love and i want to come to work  excited and happy to see my colleagues  and i totally get that here

